.TH C5XDIS 1 "2026-10-05" "c5xtools 1.0.1" "c5xtools Manual"
.SH NAME
c5xdis \- disassembler for the TMS320C5x fixed-point DSP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B c5xdis
.I image.bin
.RI [ start_word
.RI [ count_words ]]
.RB [ \-\-base
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-trace ]
.RB [ \-\-entry
.IR addr ]
.RB [ \-\-symbols
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-\-addr ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-q | \-h | \-\-version ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B c5xdis
decodes a raw TMS320C5x program image \-\- a stream of little-endian 16-bit
words \-\- back into
.BR c5xasm (1)
assembly source. Recognised instructions are decoded to their mnemonics;
anything that is not a valid instruction is emitted as a
.B .word
directive, so the printed source always re-assembles to the exact bytes it
came from.
.PP
Two modes are provided. The default is a linear sweep: every word is decoded
in order. With
.B \-\-trace
the disassembler instead follows the flow of control by recursive descent from
the entry points, separating code from data, assigning labels to branch and
call targets, tracking the data-page pointer, resolving computed jump tables
where it can, and marking the places where it cannot be certain. Trace mode
produces far more readable output for real programs.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.I image.bin
The raw image to disassemble: little-endian 16-bit words, no header. Exactly
one input is required.
.TP
.I start_word
Optional second positional argument: the word offset into the image at which
to begin. The default is the beginning of the image.
.TP
.I count_words
Optional third positional argument: how many words to process. The default is
to the end of the image.
.TP
.BI \-\-base " addr"
Treat the first word of the image as residing at word address
.I addr
(default
.BR 0 ).
This sets the addresses printed in labels and comments and the targets of
absolute branches, so the listing matches where the code actually runs.
.TP
.B \-\-trace
Perform recursive-descent analysis instead of a linear sweep: reachable code
is traced from the entry points, data is separated from instructions, labels
are generated for targets, and uncertain spots are annotated (see below).
.TP
.BI \-\-entry " addr"
Add
.I addr
as a code entry point for
.BR \-\-trace .
May be given more than once to seed several entry points (for example reset
and interrupt vectors). Implies
.BR \-\-trace .
When none is given, tracing starts at the base address.
.TP
.BI \-\-symbols " file"
Read an external symbol file to name locations. Each
.RI ' "hexaddr name" '
line attaches
.I name
to that address; an
.RI ' "entry hexaddr" '
line adds an entry point. This lets known names be supplied as input data
rather than invented. Implies
.BR \-\-trace .
.TP
.B \-\-addr
In linear mode, append the address and raw hexadecimal of each word as a
trailing comment on its line, which is convenient for correlating the listing
with a memory dump.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
Write the disassembly to
.IR file .
The default is standard output.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the one-line identity banner and the diagnostics stream, leaving only
the disassembly on standard output. Use it when the output is piped straight
into
.BR c5xasm (1).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a usage summary and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and identity banner and exit successfully.
.SH ANNOTATIONS
In
.B \-\-trace
mode each line carries a trailing comment of the form
.RI ' "; ADDR: HEX [marks]" ',
where
.I marks
may include:
.TP
.B @UNRESOLVED
a computed branch or call whose target could not be determined statically;
.TP
.B @EXT
a target that lies outside the supplied image;
.TP
.B @SUSPECT
a word that was reached as code but does not decode to a valid instruction.
.SH EXAMPLES
Disassemble an image that is loaded at address 0x0800:
.PP
.EX
c5xdis boot.bin \-\-base 0x0800
.EE
.PP
Trace-disassemble from two entry points and write the result to a file:
.PP
.EX
c5xdis app.bin \-\-trace \-\-entry 0x0800 \-\-entry 0x0808 \-o app.asm
.EE
.PP
Round-trip a region through the assembler to confirm it re-assembles exactly:
.PP
.EX
c5xdis app.bin 0 256 \-q | c5xasm /dev/stdin
.EE
.PP
Disassemble with known names supplied from a symbol file:
.PP
.EX
c5xdis app.bin \-\-symbols names.sym \-o app.asm
.EE
.SS Full toolchain flow
The three tools chain together: assemble each source to a COFF object with
.BR c5xasm (1),
link the objects into an image with
.BR c5xlnk (1),
then turn that image into a ROM or programmer format such as Intel-HEX with
.BR c5xhex (1).
.PP
Absolute build (the usual case: fixed load addresses, directly loadable),
ending in a standard Intel-HEX file:
.PP
.EX
c5xasm main.asm \-coff \-o main.obj  # source -> COFF object
c5xasm dsp.asm  \-coff \-o dsp.obj
c5xlnk main.obj dsp.obj \-o app.out  # objects -> absolute .out
c5xhex app.out \-f intel \-o app.hex  # .out -> Intel-HEX image
.EE
.PP
Relocatable build (partially link now, finish the absolute link later):
.PP
.EX
c5xasm a.asm \-coff \-o a.obj
c5xasm b.asm \-coff \-o b.obj
c5xlnk a.obj b.obj \-r \-o part.obj   # relocatable, re-linkable
c5xlnk part.obj \-o app.out          # final absolute link
c5xhex app.out \-f srec \-o app.s19   # S-record alternative
.EE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The input image could not be opened or read.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: missing input, too many arguments, or an unknown option.
.SH NOTES
Every word that is not a recognised instruction is emitted as a
.B .word
directive, so the printed source always re-assembles to exactly the input
bytes:
.B c5xdis
never loses or guesses at data, and the round trip through
.BR c5xasm (1)
is byte-for-byte.
.PP
A linear sweep is the simplest view but cannot tell code from data, so it
mis-reads any embedded table or constant as instructions. Trace mode
.RB ( \-\-trace )
instead follows the flow of control from the entry points, so it knows which
words are really reached as code, labels the branch and call targets, and
leaves everything else as data. Accurate
.B \-\-entry
points and a correct
.B \-\-base
are therefore what make trace output faithful to the original program.
.PP
Static analysis cannot always determine a computed branch target, tell whether
a target lies inside the image, or be sure a reached word is truly code. Rather
than guess, trace mode flags exactly those cases with
.BR @UNRESOLVED ,
.B @EXT
and
.B @SUSPECT
so they can be reviewed instead of silently trusted.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR c5xasm (1),
.BR c5xlnk (1),
.BR c5xhex (1)
